WEBVTT

00:00.218 --> 00:02.189
- [Voiceover] Find the total distance

00:02.189 --> 00:03.895
traveled by the particle from

00:03.895 --> 00:05.826
time t equals zero to

00:05.826 --> 00:08.548
t equals one.

00:08.548 --> 00:10.987
Always remember they didn't say
find the total displacement,

00:10.987 --> 00:13.201
they said find the total distance

00:13.201 --> 00:15.395
traveled by the particle.

00:15.395 --> 00:18.788
So if something goes to the right by one,

00:18.788 --> 00:21.328
and then goes up by one,

00:21.328 --> 00:23.644
their distance is two.

00:23.644 --> 00:25.635
And actually, and then if they go back,

00:25.635 --> 00:28.656
if they go back, over here,

00:28.656 --> 00:30.288
just let me draw a straighter line,

00:30.288 --> 00:32.056
they then go back to

00:32.056 --> 00:33.579
the original starting point.

00:33.579 --> 00:36.079
And then this distance
right here would be what?

00:36.079 --> 00:39.187
The square root of,
the square root of two,

00:39.187 --> 00:41.138
the displacement would be zero.

00:41.138 --> 00:42.661
They got back to where they started.

00:42.661 --> 00:44.978
For the distance would be one plus one

00:44.978 --> 00:47.416
plus the square root of two.

00:47.416 --> 00:49.265
So how do we figure out the total distance

00:49.265 --> 00:51.417
in this scenario right over here?

00:51.417 --> 00:53.653
Let me erase this since it's
not relevant to the problem,

00:53.653 --> 00:55.116
it's just a, for remind ourselves

00:55.116 --> 00:56.782
we're talking about distance.

00:56.782 --> 00:59.058
Well distance is equal to rate times time.

00:59.058 --> 01:01.374
Or it's equal to speed times time.

01:01.374 --> 01:03.141
So in a very small amount of time,

01:03.141 --> 01:04.747
if you want to figure out the distance,

01:04.747 --> 01:07.652
well, you could take your speed,

01:07.652 --> 01:11.573
which is the magnus magnitude
of your velocity function.

01:11.573 --> 01:13.564
If you took your speed,

01:13.564 --> 01:15.941
that's your speed right over there,

01:15.941 --> 01:19.110
and if you multiply it by a
little small change in time,

01:19.110 --> 01:20.066
that's gonna give you

01:20.066 --> 01:23.828
your infinitesimally
small change in distance

01:23.828 --> 01:26.527
over that infinitesimally
small change in time.

01:26.527 --> 01:27.746
And if you wanted to change your,

01:27.746 --> 01:29.963
if you wanted to find the total distance

01:29.963 --> 01:33.211
over a non-infinitesimal change in time,

01:33.211 --> 01:34.999
well, then you could integrate.

01:34.999 --> 01:37.072
And you can integrate those
little changes in time

01:37.072 --> 01:41.196
in this case from t equals
zero to t equals one.

01:41.196 --> 01:44.711
This is going to be the
expression for the total distance.

01:44.711 --> 01:46.357
Well what is this going to be?

01:46.357 --> 01:47.941
Well this is going to
be equal to the interval

01:47.941 --> 01:49.628
from zero to one.

01:49.628 --> 01:50.664
In the last part of the problem,

01:50.664 --> 01:54.117
we already have an
expression for our speed.

01:54.117 --> 01:58.137
Our speed we saw was this
business right over here.

01:58.137 --> 02:01.922
So it's going to be
equal to the square root,

02:01.922 --> 02:03.647
give myself a little bit more

02:03.647 --> 02:05.679
space to work with, is
going to be equal to

02:05.679 --> 02:10.559
the square root of the
x-component of the velocity,

02:10.559 --> 02:14.828
cosign of t squared squared,

02:14.828 --> 02:17.117
let me put the little
outer parenthesis there,

02:17.117 --> 02:21.404
squared, plus the y-component
of our velocity squared,

02:21.404 --> 02:24.452
the rate of change of y with
respect to time squared.

02:24.452 --> 02:29.002
So plus e to the 0.5 t,

02:29.002 --> 02:30.052
that's the rate of change of y

02:30.052 --> 02:33.148
with respect to time, or
the y-component of velocity,

02:33.148 --> 02:34.407
we're gonna square that.

02:34.407 --> 02:38.207
This is our expression for our
speed as a function of time.

02:38.207 --> 02:40.523
And then you multiply that times dt

02:40.523 --> 02:41.580
and we're gonna integrate all this

02:41.580 --> 02:43.611
and this is gonna give us our total,

02:43.611 --> 02:45.887
this is gonna give us our total distance.

02:45.887 --> 02:48.020
And lucky for us we can use our calculator

02:48.020 --> 02:52.023
in this part of the AP exam,
so let's evaluate this.

02:52.023 --> 02:56.005
So let me turn it on.

02:56.005 --> 02:57.462
Let me clear out.

02:57.462 --> 02:59.840
And so we're just going to evaluate,

02:59.840 --> 03:01.526
let's go to math,

03:01.526 --> 03:04.615
and function integral right over there,

03:04.615 --> 03:06.775
that's for evaluating definite integrals,

03:06.775 --> 03:10.596
and we want to evaluate the square root,

03:10.596 --> 03:12.904
an open parenthesis,

03:12.904 --> 03:15.240
of, I think I'm gonna have
a lot of parenthesis here,

03:15.240 --> 03:16.643
but we'll see if we can do it.

03:16.643 --> 03:20.096
So the square root of, cosign of,

03:20.096 --> 03:22.127
and I'm gonna us x as
my variable integration

